x86: harmony_pvclock — exit-count-derived paravirt clocksource (task 110)

Kernel side of the harmony deterministic hypervisor's paravirt exit-count-derived
clock page (HARMONY_PVCLOCK_ABI 1; docs/PARAVIRT-CLOCK.md in the harmony repo
is the layout authority): a kvmclock-shaped clocksource with the interpolation
deleted (.read() = a seqlock page load returning materialized V-time),
sched_clock routed through the same read, TSC marked unstable once the page is
live. Runtime-inert without the `harmony_pvclock` kernel parameter, which only
the harmony vmm appends when it offers the page — one image serves as both the
page-on and page-off measurement arm. Registration rides the hypercall
doorbell (service 7 op 1), one-shot per the ABI ruling, bracketed by two
deliberate rdtsc traps: an anchor-freshener before ringing, and the REQUIRED
post-doorbell handshake rdtsc where the host lays the canonical first stamp
(the OUT only records a pending registration — r8 handshake
ruling).

On assigned-at-exit (VM-exit-count V-time) stock hosts, rdtsc does not trap:
the host arms the registration and stamps the page at the doorbell OUT
itself, and the two deliberate rdtscs execute natively with their values
discarded — one guest flow serves both hosts. Because every native rdtsc is
then an uninterceptable nondeterministic input, the harmony_pvclock parameter
also takes the kernel off the raw TSC entirely: sched_clock holds its jiffies
fallback until the page routes it (the __use_tsc static key never enables),
the tsc-early clocksource never registers (and the harmony initcall's
mark_tsc_unstable keeps the refined tsc clocksource out), and
random_get_entropy() reads the clock page — zero before the page is live —
instead of rdtsc. The boot CPU's cyc2ns scale is seeded from cycle 0
instead of a live rdtsc, so the per-cpu conversion data (dead while
__use_tsc is off) is a pure function of tsc_khz rather than of host boot
latency. Entropy timing, printk timestamps, and timekeeping are then
pure functions of the deterministic exit stream.

The jitter RNG cannot run on such a clock: between exits time is frozen, and
the collector-priming loop in jent_entropy_collector_alloc() spins forever on
stuck measurements (outside FIPS mode the health test never breaks the loop)
before its own timer sanity checks can reject the clock. jent_mod_init
therefore declines registration when the parameter is set, and the DRBG runs
without that seed source.

This patch is Linux-kernel code (GPL-2.0), carried as a kernel diff under
consonance/harmony-linux/linux/patches/ per the repo's kernel-patch licensing exception
(patches/README.md); it is not first-party harmony source. Applied by
consonance/harmony-linux/linux/build-kernel.sh; the counter-opcode reachability gate
(scan-counter-opcodes.sh) accounts for this file's two deliberate rdtsc
reads in its reviewed allowlist.

---
Under harmony_pvclock the FPU save paths also pin MXCSR_MASK: FXSAVE and
the XSAVE variants store the host CPU's mask into every save image they
write (uninterceptably), so fxsave()/os_xsave() overwrite the field with
the frozen contract's architectural value, the signal-frame writers
(xsave_to_user_sigframe()/fxsave_to_user_sigframe()) rewrite the field in
the user-stack image after the save, and fpu__init_system_mxcsr pins
mxcsr_feature_mask to match — saved FPU state, signal frames, and kernel
copies carry no host identity on any vendor.

Under harmony_pvclock the kernel also clears the vendor-undefined AF flag
wherever RFLAGS becomes data: native_save_fl() pins the word
local_irq_save() stores, irqentry_enter() pins the exception/interrupt
frame's stored image, and do_syscall_64() pins the R11-derived pt_regs
word. AF after shifts and multiplies is architecturally undefined and the
two x86 vendors produce different values; no 64-bit-mode instruction reads
the flag, so pinned images restore without observable effect.

